7.4.2 The Error Log
-------------------

* Menu:

* error-log-configuration::      Error Log Configuration
* error-log-destination-configuration::  Default Error Log Destination Configuration
* error-log-event-fields::       Error Event Fields
* error-log-filtering::          Types of Error Log Filtering
* error-log-priority-based-filtering::  Priority-Based Error Log Filtering (log_filter_internal)
* error-log-rule-based-filtering::  Rule-Based Error Log Filtering (log_filter_dragnet)
* error-log-json::               Error Logging in JSON Format
* error-log-syslog::             Error Logging to the System Log
* error-log-format::             Error Log Output Format
* error-log-rotation::           Error Log File Flushing and Renaming

This section discusses how to configure the MySQL server for logging of
diagnostic messages to the error log.  For information about selecting
the error message character set and language, see *note
charset-errors::, and *note error-message-language::.

The error log contains a record of *note 'mysqld': mysqld. startup and
shutdown times.  It also contains diagnostic messages such as errors,
warnings, and notes that occur during server startup and shutdown, and
while the server is running.  For example, if *note 'mysqld': mysqld.
notices that a table needs to be automatically checked or repaired, it
writes a message to the error log.

Depending on error log configuration, error messages may also populate
the Performance Schema *note 'error_log':
performance-schema-error-log-table. table, to provide an SQL interface
to the log and enable its contents to be queried.  See *note
performance-schema-error-log-table::.

On some operating systems, the error log contains a stack trace if *note
'mysqld': mysqld. exits abnormally.  The trace can be used to determine
where *note 'mysqld': mysqld. exited.  See *note debugging-mysql::.

If used to start *note 'mysqld': mysqld, *note 'mysqld_safe':
mysqld-safe. may write messages to the error log.  For example, when
*note 'mysqld_safe': mysqld-safe. notices abnormal *note 'mysqld':
mysqld. exits, it restarts *note 'mysqld': mysqld. and writes a 'mysqld
restarted' message to the error log.

The following sections discuss aspects of configuring error logging.


