7.5 MySQL Components
====================

* Menu:

* component-loading::            Installing and Uninstalling Components
* obtaining-component-information::  Obtaining Component Information
* error-log-components::         Error Log Components
* query-attribute-components::   Query Attribute Components
* scheduler-component::          Scheduler Component

MySQL Server includes a component-based infrastructure for extending
server capabilities.  A component provides services that are available
to the server and other components.  (With respect to service use, the
server is a component, equal to other components.)  Components interact
with each other only through the services they provide.

MySQL distributions include several components that implement server
extensions:

   * Components for configuring error logging.  See *note error-log::,
     and *note error-log-components::.

   * A component for checking passwords.  See *note validate-password::.

   * Keyring components provide secure storage for sensitive
     information.  See *note keyring::.

   * A component that enables applications to add their own message
     events to the audit log.  See *note audit-api-message-emit::.

   * A component that implements a loadable function for accessing query
     attributes.  See *note query-attributes::.

   * A component for scheduling actively executing tasks.  See *note
     scheduler-component::.

System and status variables implemented by a component are exposed when
the component is installed and have names that begin with a
component-specific prefix.  For example, the 'log_filter_dragnet' error
log filter component implements a system variable named
'log_error_filter_rules', the full name of which is
'dragnet.log_error_filter_rules'.  To refer to this variable, use the
full name.

The following sections describe how to install and uninstall components,
and how to determine at runtime which components are installed and
obtain information about them.

For information about the internal implementation of components, see the
MySQL Server Doxygen documentation, available at
<https://dev.mysql.com/doc/index-other.html>.  For example, if you
intend to write your own components, this information is important for
understanding how components work.


