15.2.20 WITH (Common Table Expressions)
---------------------------------------

A common table expression (CTE) is a named temporary result set that
exists within the scope of a single statement and that can be referred
to later within that statement, possibly multiple times.  The following
discussion describes how to write statements that use CTEs.

   * *note common-table-expressions::

   * *note common-table-expressions-recursive::

   * *note common-table-expressions-recursion-limits::

   * *note common-table-expressions-recursive-examples::

   * *note common-table-expressions-similar-constructs::

For information about CTE optimization, see *note
derived-table-optimization::.

*Additional Resources*

These articles contain additional information about using CTEs in MySQL,
including many examples:

   * MySQL 8.0 Labs: [Recursive] Common Table Expressions in MySQL
     (CTEs)
     (https://dev.mysql.com/blog-archive/mysql-8-0-labs-recursive-common-table-expressions-in-mysql-ctes/)

