15.7.1.2 CREATE ROLE Statement
..............................

     CREATE ROLE [IF NOT EXISTS] ROLE [, ROLE ] ...

*note 'CREATE ROLE': create-role. creates one or more roles, which are
named collections of privileges.  To use this statement, you must have
the global 'CREATE ROLE' or 'CREATE USER' privilege.  When the
'read_only' system variable is enabled, *note 'CREATE ROLE':
create-role. additionally requires the 'CONNECTION_ADMIN' privilege (or
the deprecated 'SUPER' privilege).

A role when created is locked, has no password, and is assigned the
default authentication plugin.  (These role attributes can be changed
later with the *note 'ALTER USER': alter-user. statement, by users who
have the global 'CREATE USER' privilege.)

*note 'CREATE ROLE': create-role. either succeeds for all named roles or
rolls back and has no effect if any error occurs.  By default, an error
occurs if you try to create a role that already exists.  If the 'IF NOT
EXISTS' clause is given, the statement produces a warning for each named
role that already exists, rather than an error.

The statement is written to the binary log if it succeeds, but not if it
fails; in that case, rollback occurs and no changes are made.  A
statement written to the binary log includes all named roles.  If the
'IF NOT EXISTS' clause is given, this includes even roles that already
exist and were not created.

Each role name uses the format described in *note role-names::.  For
example:

     CREATE ROLE 'admin', 'developer';
     CREATE ROLE 'webapp'@'localhost';

The host name part of the role name, if omitted, defaults to ''%''.

For role usage examples, see *note roles::.


