15.7.8.8 RESTART Statement
..........................

     RESTART

This statement stops and restarts the MySQL server.  It requires the
'SHUTDOWN' privilege.

One use for *note 'RESTART': restart. is when it is not possible or
convenient to gain command-line access to the MySQL server on the server
host to restart it.  For example, *note 'SET PERSIST_ONLY':
set-variable. can be used at runtime to make configuration changes to
system variables that can be set only at server startup, but the server
must still be restarted for those changes to take effect.  The *note
'RESTART': restart. statement provides a way to do so from within client
sessions, without requiring command-line access on the server host.

*Note*:

After executing a *note 'RESTART': restart. statement, the client can
expect the current connection to be lost.  If auto-reconnect is enabled,
the connection is reestablished after the server restarts.  Otherwise,
the connection must be reestablished manually.

A successful *note 'RESTART': restart. operation requires *note
'mysqld': mysqld. to be running in an environment that has a monitoring
process available to detect a server shutdown performed for restart
purposes:

   * In the presence of a monitoring process, *note 'RESTART': restart.
     causes *note 'mysqld': mysqld. to terminate such that the
     monitoring process can determine that it should start a new *note
     'mysqld': mysqld. instance.

   * If no monitoring process is present, *note 'RESTART': restart.
     fails with an error.

These platforms provide the necessary monitoring support for the *note
'RESTART': restart. statement:

   * Windows, when *note 'mysqld': mysqld. is started as a Windows
     service or standalone.  (*note 'mysqld': mysqld. forks, and one
     process acts as a monitor to the other, which acts as the server.)

   * Unix and Unix-like systems that use systemd or *note 'mysqld_safe':
     mysqld-safe. to manage *note 'mysqld': mysqld.

To configure a monitoring environment such that *note 'mysqld': mysqld.
enables the *note 'RESTART': restart. statement:

  1. Set the 'MYSQLD_PARENT_PID' environment variable to the value of
     the process ID of the process that starts *note 'mysqld': mysqld,
     before starting *note 'mysqld': mysqld.

  2. When *note 'mysqld': mysqld. performs a shutdown due to use of the
     *note 'RESTART': restart. statement, it returns exit code 16.

  3. When the monitoring process detects an exit code of 16, it starts
     *note 'mysqld': mysqld. again.  Otherwise, it exits.

Here is a minimal example as implemented in the 'bash' shell:

     #!/bin/bash

     export MYSQLD_PARENT_PID=$$

     export MYSQLD_RESTART_EXIT=16

     while true ; do
       bin/mysqld MYSQLD OPTIONS HERE
       if [ $? -ne $MYSQLD_RESTART_EXIT ]; then
         break
       fi
     done

On Windows, the forking used to implement *note 'RESTART': restart.
makes determining the server process to attach to for debugging more
difficult.  To alleviate this, starting the server with '--gdb'
suppresses forking, in addition to its other actions done to set up a
debugging environment.  In non-debug settings, '--no-monitor' may be
used for the sole purpose of suppressing forking the monitor process.
For a server started with either '--gdb' or '--no-monitor', executing
*note 'RESTART': restart. causes the server to simply exit without
restarting.

The 'Com_restart' status variable tracks the number of *note 'RESTART':
restart. statements.  Because status variables are initialized for each
server startup and do not persist across restarts, 'Com_restart'
normally has a value of zero, but can be nonzero if *note 'RESTART':
restart. statements were executed but failed.


