20 Group Replication
********************

* Menu:

* group-replication-background::  Group Replication Background
* group-replication-getting-started::  Getting Started
* group-replication-requirements-and-limitations::  Requirements and Limitations
* group-replication-monitoring::  Monitoring Group Replication
* group-replication-operations::  Group Replication Operations
* group-replication-security::   Group Replication Security
* group-replication-performance::  Group Replication Performance and Troubleshooting
* group-replication-upgrade::    Upgrading Group Replication
* group-replication-options::    Group Replication Variables
* group-replication-frequently-asked-questions::  Frequently Asked Questions

This chapter explains MySQL Group Replication and how to install,
configure and monitor groups.  MySQL Group Replication enables you to
create elastic, highly-available, fault-tolerant replication topologies.

Groups can operate in a single-primary mode with automatic primary
election, where only one server accepts updates at a time.
Alternatively, groups can be deployed in multi-primary mode, where all
servers can accept updates, even if they are issued concurrently.

There is a built-in group membership service that keeps the view of the
group consistent and available for all servers at any given point in
time.  Servers can leave and join the group and the view is updated
accordingly.  Sometimes servers can leave the group unexpectedly, in
which case the failure detection mechanism detects this and notifies the
group that the view has changed.  This is all automatic.

Group Replication guarantees that the database service is continuously
available.  However, it is important to understand that if one of the
group members becomes unavailable, the clients connected to that group
member must be redirected, or failed over, to a different server in the
group, using a connector, load balancer, router, or some form of
middleware.  Group Replication does not have an inbuilt method to do
this.  For example, see MySQL Router 8.0
(https://dev.mysql.com/doc/mysql-router/8.0/en/).

Group Replication is provided as a plugin to MySQL Server.  You can
follow the instructions in this chapter to configure the plugin on each
of the server instances that you want in the group, start up the group,
and monitor and administer the group.  An alternative way to deploy a
group of MySQL server instances is by using InnoDB Cluster.

*Tip*:

To deploy multiple instances of MySQL, you can use InnoDB Cluster
(https://dev.mysql.com/doc/mysql-shell/8.0/en/mysql-innodb-cluster.html)
which enables you to easily administer a group of MySQL server instances
in MySQL Shell (https://dev.mysql.com/doc/mysql-shell/8.0/en/).  InnoDB
Cluster wraps MySQL Group Replication in a programmatic environment that
enables you easily deploy a cluster of MySQL instances to achieve high
availability.  In addition, InnoDB Cluster interfaces seamlessly with
MySQL Router (https://dev.mysql.com/doc/mysql-router/8.0/en/), which
enables your applications to connect to the cluster without writing your
own failover process.  For similar use cases that do not require high
availability, however, you can use InnoDB ReplicaSet
(https://dev.mysql.com/doc/mysql-shell/8.0/en/mysql-innodb-replicaset.html).
Installation instructions for MySQL Shell can be found here
(https://dev.mysql.com/doc/mysql-shell/8.0/en/mysql-shell-install.html).

The chapter is structured as follows:

   * *note group-replication-background:: provides an introduction to
     groups and how Group Replication works.

   * *note group-replication-getting-started:: explains how to configure
     multiple MySQL Server instances to create a group.

   * *note group-replication-requirements-and-limitations:: explains
     architecture and setup requirements and limitations for Group
     Replication.

   * *note group-replication-monitoring:: explains how to monitor a
     group.

   * *note group-replication-operations:: explains how to work with a
     group.

   * *note group-replication-security:: explains how to secure a group.

   * *note group-replication-performance:: explains how to fine tune
     performance for a group.

   * *note group-replication-upgrade:: explains how to upgrade a group.

   * *note group-replication-options:: is a reference for the system
     variables specific to Group Replication.

   * *note group-replication-frequently-asked-questions:: provides
     answers to some technical questions about deploying and operating
     Group Replication.


