20.6.3.4 Providing Replication User Credentials Securely
........................................................

To supply the user credentials for the replication user, you can set
them permanently as the credentials for the 'group_replication_recovery'
channel, using a *note 'CHANGE REPLICATION SOURCE TO':
change-replication-source-to. | *note 'CHANGE MASTER TO':
change-master-to. statement.  Alternatively, from MySQL 8.0.21, you can
specify them on the *note 'START GROUP_REPLICATION':
start-group-replication. statement each time Group Replication is
started.  User credentials specified on *note 'START GROUP_REPLICATION':
start-group-replication. take precedence over any user credentials that
have been set using a *note 'CHANGE REPLICATION SOURCE TO':
change-replication-source-to. | *note 'CHANGE MASTER TO':
change-master-to. statement.

User credentials set using *note 'CHANGE REPLICATION SOURCE TO':
change-replication-source-to. | *note 'CHANGE MASTER TO':
change-master-to. are stored in plain text in the replication metadata
repositories on the server, but user credentials specified on *note
'START GROUP_REPLICATION': start-group-replication. are saved in memory
only, and are removed by a *note 'STOP GROUP_REPLICATION':
stop-group-replication. statement or server shutdown.  Using *note
'START GROUP_REPLICATION': start-group-replication. to specify the user
credentials therefore helps to secure the Group Replication servers
against unauthorized access.  However, this method is not compatible
with starting Group Replication automatically, as specified by the
'group_replication_start_on_boot' system variable.

If you want to set the user credentials permanently using a *note
'CHANGE REPLICATION SOURCE TO': change-replication-source-to. | *note
'CHANGE MASTER TO': change-master-to. statement, issue this statement on
the member that is going to join the group:

     mysql> CHANGE MASTER TO MASTER_USER='REC_SSL_USER', MASTER_PASSWORD='PASSWORD'
                 FOR CHANNEL 'group_replication_recovery';

     Or from MySQL 8.0.23:
     mysql> CHANGE REPLICATION SOURCE TO SOURCE_USER='REC_SSL_USER', SOURCE_PASSWORD='PASSWORD'
                 FOR CHANNEL 'group_replication_recovery';

To supply the user credentials on *note 'START GROUP_REPLICATION':
start-group-replication, issue this statement when starting Group
Replication for the first time, or after a server restart:

     mysql> START GROUP_REPLICATION USER='REC_SSL_USER', PASSWORD='PASSWORD';

*Important*:

If you switch to using *note 'START GROUP_REPLICATION':
start-group-replication. to specify user credentials on a server that
previously supplied the credentials using *note 'CHANGE REPLICATION
SOURCE TO': change-replication-source-to. | *note 'CHANGE MASTER TO':
change-master-to, you must complete the following steps to get the
security benefits of this change.

  1. Stop Group Replication on the group member using a *note 'STOP
     GROUP_REPLICATION': stop-group-replication. statement.  Although it
     is possible to take the following two steps while Group Replication
     is running, you need to restart Group Replication to implement the
     changes.

  2. Set the value of the 'group_replication_start_on_boot' system
     variable to 'OFF' (the default is 'ON').

  3. Remove the distributed recovery credentials from the replica status
     tables by issuing this statement:

          mysql> CHANGE MASTER TO MASTER_USER='', MASTER_PASSWORD=''
                      FOR CHANNEL 'group_replication_recovery';

          Or from MySQL 8.0.23:
          mysql> CHANGE REPLICATION SOURCE TO SOURCE_USER='', SOURCE_PASSWORD=''
                      FOR CHANNEL 'group_replication_recovery';

  4. Restart Group Replication on the group member using a *note 'START
     GROUP_REPLICATION': start-group-replication. statement that
     specifies the distributed recovery user credentials.

Without these steps, the credentials remain stored in the replica status
tables, and can also be transferred to other group members during remote
cloning operations for distributed recovery.  The
'group_replication_recovery' channel could then be inadvertently started
with the stored credentials, on either the original member or members
that were cloned from it.  An automatic start of Group Replication on
server boot (including after a remote cloning operation) would use the
stored user credentials, and they would also be used if an operator did
not specify the distributed recovery credentials as part of *note 'START
GROUP_REPLICATION': start-group-replication.


