20.8 Upgrading Group Replication
================================

* Menu:

* group-replication-online-upgrade-combining-versions::  Combining Different Member Versions in a Group
* group-replication-offline-upgrade::  Group Replication Offline Upgrade
* group-replication-online-upgrade::  Group Replication Online Upgrade

This section explains how to upgrade a Group Replication setup.  The
basic process of upgrading members of a group is the same as upgrading
stand-alone instances, see *note upgrading:: for the actual process of
doing upgrade and types available.  Choosing between an in-place or
logical upgrade depends on the amount of data stored in the group.
Usually an in-place upgrade is faster, and therefore is recommended.
You should also consult *note replication-upgrade::.

While you are in the process of upgrading an online group, in order to
maximize availability, you might need to have members with different
MySQL Server versions running at the same time.  Group Replication
includes compatibility policies that enable you to safely combine
members running different versions of MySQL in the same group during the
upgrade procedure.  Depending on your group, the effects of these
policies might affect the order in which you should upgrade group
members.  For details, see *note
group-replication-online-upgrade-combining-versions::.

If your group can be taken fully offline see *note
group-replication-offline-upgrade::.  If your group needs to remain
online, as is common with production deployments, see *note
group-replication-online-upgrade:: for the different approaches
available for upgrading a group with minimal downtime.


