28.4.11 The INFORMATION_SCHEMA INNODB_FIELDS Table
--------------------------------------------------

The *note 'INNODB_FIELDS': information-schema-innodb-fields-table. table
provides metadata about the key columns (fields) of 'InnoDB' indexes.

For related usage information and examples, see *note
innodb-information-schema-system-tables::.

The *note 'INNODB_FIELDS': information-schema-innodb-fields-table. table
has these columns:

   * 'INDEX_ID'

     An identifier for the index associated with this key field; the
     same value as 'INNODB_INDEXES.INDEX_ID'.

   * 'NAME'

     The name of the original column from the table; the same value as
     'INNODB_COLUMNS.NAME'.

   * 'POS'

     The ordinal position of the key field within the index, starting
     from 0 and incrementing sequentially.  When a column is dropped,
     the remaining columns are reordered so that the sequence has no
     gaps.

*Example*

     mysql> SELECT * FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.INNODB_FIELDS WHERE INDEX_ID = 117\G
     *************************** 1. row ***************************
     INDEX_ID: 117
         NAME: col1
          POS: 0

*Notes*

   * You must have the 'PROCESS' privilege to query this table.

   * Use the 'INFORMATION_SCHEMA' *note 'COLUMNS':
     information-schema-columns-table. table or the *note 'SHOW
     COLUMNS': show-columns. statement to view additional information
     about the columns of this table, including data types and default
     values.


