29.12.20 Performance Schema Summary Tables
------------------------------------------

* Menu:

* performance-schema-wait-summary-tables::  Wait Event Summary Tables
* performance-schema-stage-summary-tables::  Stage Summary Tables
* performance-schema-statement-summary-tables::  Statement Summary Tables
* performance-schema-statement-histogram-summary-tables::  Statement Histogram Summary Tables
* performance-schema-transaction-summary-tables::  Transaction Summary Tables
* performance-schema-objects-summary-global-by-type-table::  Object Wait Summary Table
* performance-schema-file-summary-tables::  File I/O Summary Tables
* performance-schema-table-wait-summary-tables::  Table I/O and Lock Wait Summary Tables
* performance-schema-socket-summary-tables::  Socket Summary Tables
* performance-schema-memory-summary-tables::  Memory Summary Tables
* performance-schema-error-summary-tables::  Error Summary Tables
* performance-schema-status-variable-summary-tables::  Status Variable Summary Tables

Summary tables provide aggregated information for terminated events over
time.  The tables in this group summarize event data in different ways.

Each summary table has grouping columns that determine how to group the
data to be aggregated, and summary columns that contain the aggregated
values.  Tables that summarize events in similar ways often have similar
sets of summary columns and differ only in the grouping columns used to
determine how events are aggregated.

Summary tables can be truncated with *note 'TRUNCATE TABLE':
truncate-table.  Generally, the effect is to reset the summary columns
to 0 or 'NULL', not to remove rows.  This enables you to clear collected
values and restart aggregation.  That might be useful, for example,
after you have made a runtime configuration change.  Exceptions to this
truncation behavior are noted in individual summary table sections.

